      "id": 1,
      "text": "Bromley was named after the English town of Bromley, reflecting its founder’s hometown.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.cityofbromley.com/HistoryofBromley.aspx"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The settlement began in 1784 when Prettyman Perry received a 2,000‑acre land grant from the U.S. government.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.cityofbromley.com/HistoryofBromley.aspx"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Two years after its founding, the First German Reformed Church was established, now known as Immanuel United Church of Christ.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.cityofbromley.com/HistoryofBromley.aspx"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Landmark, a historic home built by the Merry family, still stands today on Shelby Street.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.cityofbromley.com/HistoryofBromley.aspx"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Bromley has endured periodic flooding throughout its history, shaping its development along the Ohio River.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.cityofbromley.com/HistoryofBromley.aspx"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Bromley is a home‑rule city on the Ohio River’s south bank, part of the Cincinnati metropolitan area.",
